.TH FUZZYGENE 1 2000-1月 "fuzzygene_dose_response" "fuzzygene_dose_response"
.SH NAME
fuzzygene_dose_response \- Dose-response curve of one virtual perturbation.
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fIfuzzygene_dose_response(\fBfuzzygene\fR as <generic> FuzzyGeneModel, 
\fBgene\fR as string, 
\fBdoses\fR as double = \fB<NULL>\fR, 
\fBsteps\fR as integer = 60, 
\fBtop\fR as integer = 50, 
[\fB<Environment>\fR]);\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Dose-response curve of one virtual perturbation.
.PP
.SH OPTIONS
.PP
\fBfuzzygene\fB \fR\- a trained (or restored) model. 
.PP
.PP
\fBgene\fB \fR\- the target gene of the virtual perturbation. 
.PP
.PP
\fBdoses\fB \fR\- dose points in [0, 1]; \fBNULL\fR uses 5 evenly spaced points. 
.PP
.PP
\fBsteps\fB \fR\- iteration budget of each solve. 
.PP
.PP
\fBtop\fB \fR\- number of genes to return; 0 means all. 
.PP
.PP
\fBenv\fB \fR\- the R# runtime environment used to report errors. 
.PP
.SH VALUE
.PP
a long data.frame \fBdose\fR / \fBclamp_value\fR / \fBgene\fR / \fBfold_change\fR / \fBpercent_change\fR / \fBz_score\fR / \fBsignificant\fR
.PP
.SH DETAILS
.PP
Dose `d` runs from 0 (pin the gene at its baseline) to 1 (full knockout); the clamped
 value is `baseline x (1 - d)`. Note that `d = 0` is not the same as "no
 perturbation": the gene is held fixed while the wildtype trajectory drifts away from the
 baseline, which is why its own fold change is 0 at `d = 0` but other genes can still
 move.
 


 


 Only the top genes are returned (ranked by \fB|z|\fR at the largest dose) to keep the
 data.frame small; pass \fBtop = 0\fR for all 18k genes.
 


 
\fB
 let r = FuzzyGene::fuzzygene_dose_response(model, "TPPP3", doses = c(0, 0.5, 1));
 print(r);
 
\fR`
.PP
